{"action": "diagnose", "why": "The evidence agrees: the provider stopped answering around 02:42, and our 503s and 10 s latency follow directly from that; a further call would not change the fix.", "diagnosis": {"hypothesis": "fake-provider, which stands for the outside payment provider, suddenly stopped answering POST /v1/payments and /v1/refunds at about 02:42. Its container still reports running and healthy, so it is hanging rather than down. payments-api waits 3 s per provider call and tries 3 times, so each request now takes about 9 to 16 s and then gets a 503 ('provider unavailable; payment left pending'). That gives the 74% 5xx rate and the 10 s p95. Until the stall, provider calls returned 200 in about 5 ms (p95), with only occasional 500s. The change was abrupt, not a gradual overload. No deploy lines up with it: the whole stack was created at 02:39:46. payments-api's own health check now fails, most likely because its workers are tied up waiting on the provider. That is an inference I did not confirm. Still open: why the provider stalled, which we cannot see from our side. Also open: whether the timed-out requests reached the provider and were charged there. Any of these pending payments or refunds may exist on the provider's side. I did not run the money check.", "evidence": [{"call_id": "c1", "shows": "POST /payments: 201s fall from about 2.5/s to 0 and 503s jump to about 2.5/s at 02:42:09, confirming the alert. 409s also rise slightly."}, {"call_id": "c2", "shows": "payments-api's provider calls (200 at about 3/s, 500 at about 0.2/s) both drop to 0 completed calls at 02:42:12. The provider stopped responding."}, {"call_id": "c3", "shows": "Every recent payments-api ERROR reads 'provider unavailable; payment/refund left pending' with '/v1/payments (or /v1/refunds) failed after 3 attempts: ReadTimeout'. This starts at 02:42:09."}, {"call_id": "c6", "shows": "In trace pmt_000276, three POSTs to fake-provider:8000/v1/payments each hit ReadTimeout after about 3.0 s. The request took 15.9 s and returned 503. The Redis and Postgres spans took under 1 ms, so those dependencies are not the cause."}, {"call_id": "c7", "shows": "Provider-call p95 was steady at about 5 ms through 02:42:05, then has no data. The stall was sudden, with no gradual slowdown."}, {"call_id": "c4", "shows": "All containers were created together at 02:39:46. No deploy or settings change coincides with the 02:42 onset."}, {"call_id": "c5", "shows": "fake-provider is running and 'healthy' with 0 restarts, so it is hung rather than crashed. payments-api is 'unhealthy': its health-check request itself fails."}], "confidence": 0.75, "proposed_fix": {"kind": "other", "target": "fake-provider (external payment provider) / payments-api provider client", "summary": "Escalate to the provider: their payments and refunds API is hanging. On our side, stop the pile-up while it lasts: add a circuit breaker or fail fast on provider timeouts, cutting the 3x3 s retry loop, so payments-api stays responsive and its health check recovers. Do not retry pending payments under new idempotency keys. When the provider recovers, reconcile every payment and refund left pending since 02:42, keeping the same idempotency keys and checking the provider's records and webhooks. Then run money_check to confirm our books match the provider's."}, "blast_radius": "Failing fast changes payments-api's behavior for every payment and refund while the breaker is open. Callers get 503 sooner, and payments that might have succeeded on a slow provider get rejected. Reconciling the pending payments touches payment and refund state. If it is done wrong, without idempotency keys or provider lookups, it risks double charges or missed bookings.", "rollback_plan": "The breaker or timeout change is a config change: revert it to the previous retry and timeout settings and restart payments-api. Do the reconciliation from the provider's records. Change nothing on the provider's side, and leave rows pending if their state is unclear."}}}
```
